# `golem` (runtime)

`ObserveReplication` registers any number of observers (atomic snapshot list, removable at any time) that run on the tick goroutine after the replication flush, before interest/visibility/owner filtering, with spawned/changed/removed IDs and `Moved` (`EntitySpaceMove{ID, From, To}`, straight from `FlushResult.Moves`). Inside the callback, flushed masks and `FullUpdate`/`MarshalDeltaMask` are tick-coherent; observers must not block or retain frame slices. It costs one atomic load with no observers. `OnUpdates` stays a single slot for game code.

`CreateEntityWithSetup(e, setup, owner...)` is startup/tick-owned and rejects physics callbacks, active frames, restoration and shutdown. It assigns an ID, binds the server and registers schema colliders, then calls `EntitySetup` while `Get`/`Owner` can resolve the provisional entity. Enumeration, ticking, snapshots and replication exclude it until atomic publication followed by ordinary `OnSpawn`. Setup errors detach successful physics/navigation attachments, unregister colliders and discard the provisional entry without `OnSpawn`, `OnRemove` or lifecycle output. A returned rollback function releases caller-owned resources on failure; errors are joined. Setup panics release server-owned resources and continue unwinding. Setup cannot change identity, advance simulation or mutate unrelated state; callback-owned resources acquired before a panic require callback-local cleanup. Consumed IDs are not recycled. `CreateEntity` retains its existing behavior. `LoadSnapshotStrict` supplies separate startup-only entity restoration without ordinary spawn hooks; physics-configured hosts use paired recovery.

**Spaces:** `SpaceID` aliases `registry.SpaceID`; the registry owns each entity's space. `CreateEntity` / `CreateEntityWithSetup` are space-0 wrappers of `CreateEntityIn(space, e, owner...)` / `CreateEntityWithSetupIn(space, e, setup, owner...)`, which place the entity before collider registration and `OnSpawn` (setup observes the provisional space). `AvatarOptions.Space` selects the avatar's space. `EntitySpace(id)` reads it. `SetEntitySpace(id, space)` is `MoveToSpace(SpaceTransfer{To, Entities: {id}})` without `RebuildPhysics`, so it moves plain, collider and navigation-attached entities but rejects physics-attached ones; every single-entity `MoveToSpace` keeps the historical "SetEntitySpace" diagnostics. `checkSpaceMutable` returns `ErrServerNotRunning` while stopping and an error wrapping `p.ErrBusy` while `transferring` (a transfer's rebuild/commit), `simulatingSpaces` (the per-space step loop while several runtimes exist, so no entity is simulated in two spaces per tick), `collisionStepping` (the whole legacy collision phase, 2D and 3D steps plus `onContact`/`onContact3D`/`dispatchContactEvents`, so a contact callback cannot move an entity between the 2D and 3D steps; one flag write per tick) or `servicesQuiescent` fails for any space (physics `mutable()`: steps, callbacks, restoration, fault; navigation frame/restoration) — callbacks use `Post`. `entitySetupAllowed` also rejects while `transferring || simulatingSpaces`; `attachPhysics` / `attachNavigation` and the public `DetachPhysics` / `DetachNavigation` (now returning an error) reject while `transferring` (`transferBusy`), and `DeleteEntity` while `transferring` appends to `transferDeletes`, applied by `finishTransfer` after the transfer (RebuildPhysics and MoveShapes must not call Server APIs). Batches reject duplicates; every entity must be published (`Registry.PublishedSpace`), not deleting, not awaiting navigation reattachment (`navReattachPending`, an error wrapping `p.ErrBusy`, see step 6), without a deletion queued from a physics callback (`physicsService.pendingDeletes`: entity → registry incarnation, set by `queueDelete`, which dedupes, queues nothing for unknown IDs and whose command deletes only while `Registry.Incarnation` still matches; cleared by that command for its own incarnation, by the normal `DeleteEntity` path, `stop` and restore) and not attached to a body of loaded geometry (`physicsService.geometryOwns`), and the destination must have physics plus `RebuildPhysics` for physics-attached entities, surface navigation for navigation-attached ones, and no staged agent may arrive without a body in a physics space. Plain batches move atomically under `interestMu` through `Registry.SetSpaces`. **Transfer** (`space_transfer.go`): validate everything, then call `RebuildPhysics(dst.world, []PhysicsTransferSource{Entity, From, Body, Config, Controller})` once (sources synchronized, Entities order) while source bodies exist; `Controller` is the source's `p.CharacterControllerReader` policy with `Velocity` cleared (zero without a controller or without the reader); admit each returned body like `AttachPhysics` (not attached anywhere, `foreignPhysicsBody`, valid state/owner, same `MotionMode` as the source, `checkTransferController` — when both worlds implement the reader, the same controller configuration (all fields but `Velocity`, which is runtime intent) and no gained or lost controller; skipped when either world lacks the reader — navigation mode, `validatePlacement` with the entity's policy, `OwnerBinder`). Any failure destroys every returned, unattached body (`p.Deleted`) and returns with nothing changed; after the callback `transferUnchanged` re-checks every entity's published space, deletion state, source attachments and identity by registry incarnation (captured in `colliderMove.incarnation` and `physicsAttachment.incarnation`; never compare `Entity` values with `==`, they may be non-comparable), rolling back the same way. Then `preflightTransferSources` (physics moves only) requires each source service `mutable()`, `world.Metrics().Fault == nil` (Box3D `Body` skips `guard()`) and `world.Body(h)` alive with `Handle == h`, so the detach loop cannot fail on a body the callback destroyed or on a faulted world. Then `SpaceTransfer.MoveShapes(to, []ShapeMove{Entity, From, Position})` runs (when set and some entity changes space; `transferShapeMoves`: Position is the rebuilt anchor, else `Position3D`, else `(x, y, 0)`), followed by `transferUnchanged` again. Up to here a rejection changes nothing: `rollbackTransferBodies` destroys the returned bodies and `faultServices` the destination only if that destruction fails. `spaceRuntime.faultServices(err)` sets the physics fault when unset (Run ends at the next drain) and closes navigation like a failed restore (`restoring`, `restoreErr`); a navigation-only space does not end Run but stays not ready and blocks moves and setup until a navigation restore completes. Commit order (keep it): defer source traversal-cancel callbacks (`frameCallbacks`); (1) detach every source, `physicsService.detach(id, p.Teleported)` (destroys body and joints, drops trajectory and source navigation) then source navigation; on the first failure `faultServices` the source and the destination, synchronize the services that already lost bodies, run the deferred callbacks, destroy the rebuilt bodies and return with registry and colliders unchanged (entities detached earlier have lost their source bodies and MoveShapes already ran; the fault ends Run); (2) `synchronizeRetired`: bump `retireEpoch` and `Synchronize()` once per source physics service that destroyed bodies (Box3D queries/captures stay busy otherwise; a failure `faultServices` that space, the commit continues); (3) `SetSpaces` under `interestMu` (its unreachable failure faults the destination and every retired space); (4) `applyColliderMoves`; (5) install the new physics attachment (same writers/config, fresh policy generation, local anchor from the new body) and write the new anchor as the entity position; (6) put the moved navigation entities in `navReattachPending` (removed by a `defer` after step 7, panic-safe) and run the deferred callbacks with `transferring == false` and the registry already moved, so a callback's `MoveToSpace` of such an entity returns `ErrBusy` instead of moving it without an agent; (7) `navigationService.addAgent` with the same config and no intent, only if the entity is still published in `To`, the destination has no agent and is not deleting it, and a staged agent still has its destination body (`addAgent` does not check duplicates). The collider-only fast path validates the same way before `SetSpaces`; with `MoveShapes` it sets `transferring` around the hook (deletes deferred to `finishTransfer`) and re-runs `transferUnchanged`. Queued commands carry the `retireEpoch` they were queued at; each successful transfer detach records the destroyed source body in `physicsService.retired` at the epoch `synchronizeRetired` then advances to, and `drain` drops (does not fault) a command only when `errors.As` finds a `*p.StaleHandleError` whose handle is retired at an epoch later than the command's; anything else, including an untyped `ErrStale` or a stale collider/joint handle of a moved body, faults. `retired` is cleared when a drain leaves the queue empty, on restore and on stop. Not carried: trajectories, contact history, routes/destinations, pending forces and controller velocity intent, joints to unmoved bodies; queued source commands still run in the source world. Legacy schema colliders move with the batch: `planColliderMoves` validates every transfer first through the shared `checkColliderRemove` / `planColliderAdd` (a destination layer helper must define the collider's layer; every helper the move removes from or adds to must be `Bound()`, since `Layers.Add`/`Remove` panic otherwise; no helper drops the collider, as `CreateEntity` skips registration; paired physics restore runs the same checks before its first commit), then the registry move, then `applyColliderMoves` unregisters from the old space's helpers, re-adds the provider's current shape on the new space's and immediately `Update`s the destination runtime backend with the entity position (as the next collision step would; skipped for physics-attached entities), so same-tick queries see it at its real position. That update needs no physics check: an entity with a schema collider is never physics-attached (`attachPhysics` and paired restore reject `ColliderProvider`/`ColliderProvider3D`), so keep that invariant if either rule changes. Shapes the game registers on a backend itself move only through `MoveShapes`, which finds backends with the read-only `Server.CollisionBackend` / `CollisionBackend3D` and `Space.CollisionBackend` / `CollisionBackend3D` getters (they never create a runtime); on a hook error the hook must leave its shapes unchanged and the engine changes nothing. The physics step identifies attached entities by `physicsAttachment.incarnation` (set by `attachPhysics`, carried by transfers, refreshed after `ReplaceWithoutHooks` in paired restore), not by `Entity` equality. `RebuildPhysics` ownership: rollback destroys only the returned bodies (and their joints/colliders); the callback owns shapes and anything else it created or did not return. An entity's physics and navigation attachments always live in its own space's services.

**Space runtimes** (`space_runtime.go`): `spaceRuntime{id, collision, collision3D, layers, layers3D, navBackend, navigation, physics}` holds one space's services. Space 0's is embedded as `Server.space0` (present even on a zero `Server`); others live in `Server.spaces` plus the ascending `spaceOrder` slice, created lazily by `ensureRuntime` when a `Space` handle setter installs a non-nil service (queries and nil setters never create one). `runtime(space)` and `runtimeOf(entityID)` (unknown entities resolve to space 0; no lock while `SpacesInUse` is false) return nil for spaces without services, and every `spaceRuntime` method is nil-receiver safe. `physicsService.space` / `navigationService.space` point back at the owning runtime: the physics step prepares, validates, commits and aborts its sibling navigation frame (`navigationService.prepareFrame` / `validateFrame` / `validateResults` / `commitResults` / `abortFrame`), detach reaches the sibling navigation, trajectories read sibling safety, and geometry builds carry their runtime. Fault, readiness, restoring, command queues, geometry leases and navigation path/build pools, build keys, frames and metrics are per space. `Server.physics` / `Server.navigation` are aliases of `space0.physics` / `space0.navigation`, assigned together in `setPhysicsWorld` / `setSurfaceNavigation` (lifecycle-checked under `lifeMu`: they return an error once `Run` has started, for every space); non-zero spaces' navigation services are also recorded in `navigationBySpace` (lifeMu) so `Space.NavigationStats` reads counters concurrently without the tick-owned runtime map. Paired physics/navigation checkpoints cover every serviced space (`servicedRuntimes`; `checkpointRuntimes` rejects no services or a space without physics with a configuration error, for capture and restore alike, before any `ErrBusy` check): one `PhysicsSpaceCheckpoint` section per space in version 2, top-level fields in version 1 when only space 0 is serviced (see `subsystem-golem-snapshot.mdc`). `Space(id).CaptureNavigationCheckpoint` / `RestoreNavigationIntents` act per space; the `Server` methods act on space 0. Persistence with physics (`anyPhysics`, any space; surface navigation alone does not count): `SaveContentSnapshot`, `LoadContentSnapshot` and `LoadSnapshotStrict` reject; legacy `SaveSnapshot` / `SaveSnapshotOnTick` / `LoadSnapshot` do not check and save or recreate (through `CreateEntityIn`) entities only, without bodies, bindings or navigation; paired checkpoints are the physics path. Server collision/query/2D-nav methods delegate to `space0`'s methods so there is one code path; production entity-keyed code must resolve `runtimeOf`, never `space0` (review grep). Runtimes are tick-owned. **Setup rules:** physics and surface navigation are lifecycle-checked (error once `Run` started). Legacy collision backends, layer helpers and 2D nav backends may be set before `Run` or on the tick goroutine; that is documented, not enforced. **Services are never shared:** every setter (Server space-0 setters and `Space` handle setters) runs `serviceOwner` before `ensureRuntime`, comparing the slot of every other runtime by pointer identity (`sameService`: same dynamic type and non-nil pointer/map/chan; other kinds are never shared and never compared with `==`, which can panic). Physics and surface navigation return an error; the five legacy setters (2D/3D backend, 2D/3D layers, 2D nav backend), which have no error result, panic via `mustUnshared` (generated `EnableCollision*` / `EnableSpaceCollision*` propagate the panic and store `Runtime.Layers` only after the setters). A collision backend also serves the space whose helper is bound to it (`Layers.Backend`, read through `layersBackendOf` / `layers3DBackendOf`): the backend setters also refuse a backend bound to another space's helper, and the layers setters (`mustLayersUnshared`) also refuse a helper whose backend is another space's backend or another space's helper's backend (`mustBackendUnshared`). Generated code installs the backend before the helper, so the backend setter's checks guarantee the layers setter cannot panic after it. `Server.planColliderAdd(rt, e)` rechecks at use time (`helperBackend`), since `Bind` may run after installation: the helper's backend must be the space's own pointer-like backend when it has one and never another space's backend. `Server.registerCollider` (CreateEntity, CreateEntityWithSetup, LoadSnapshotStrict and the other create paths) applies the same `helperBackend` check to a bound helper and panics before registering any shape, matching those paths' existing panic for an unbound helper (`Layers.Add`). The collision backend setters treat a typed nil as nil (`nilService`), and `MustCollisionBackend*` panic on it. Re-installing a service in its own space is allowed, and clearing a slot with nil releases it. The `Space` handle (`space.go`) offers `ID`, `Sessions`, `BroadcastServerEvent`, `World`, `PushWorldData`, the four collision setters, every `spatial.go` query, `SetNavBackend` / `FindPath` / `SetNavWalkable` (`nav_api.go`), and the context-free physics/surface-navigation methods (`space_services.go`: `SetPhysicsWorld`, `OnPhysicsContact`, `ObservePhysicsFrames`, `QueuePhysicsCommand`, `QueuePhysicsGeometry`, `ActivatePhysicsGeometry`, `PhysicsGeometry`, `PendingPhysicsGeometry`, `PhysicsReady`, `SetSurfaceNavigation`, `NavigationReady`, `NavigationStats`, build queue/cancel/forget/status, restore begin/complete and the four `NavigationFrame` methods; a frame of another space is foreign). Entity-keyed physics/navigation APIs stay on `Server` and resolve `runtimeOf`; `DeleteEntity` defers only during its own space's accepted-frame callbacks, and entity setup requires every space quiescent. `NavAgent.SetDestination` paths on `runtimeOf` of its bound value's `EntityID` (space 0 without one) and records the server, entity and space; It also records `Registry.SpaceMoveGeneration`; `Tick`, `HasPath`, `NextWaypoint` and `RemainingDistance` drop the path (`ResetPath`) once the entity's current space or move generation differs, so a round trip (0 → 5 → 0) before the next `Tick` also drops it (`SpacesInUse` false means space 0, and the generation is 0 without a lock while no entity ever moved, so this is two atomic loads in single-space games). The manual `Step(dt, x, y)` cannot detect moves.

**Session spaces:** a session's effective space is its FOI anchor's space, else its avatar's, else `SetSessionSpace` (cleared by 0), else 0; an FOI session whose anchor is missing keeps the interest manager's last space, seeded from the avatar/explicit space when the FOI is first assigned. `SessionSpace` reports the effective space. `sentSpace` is the space the known set follows: it starts at 0 and changes only when a replication pass plans a transition, under the same `interestMu` section as that pass's known-set decision, so every known set lies inside its session's `sentSpace`. Planning also records the old space in `spaceAnnouncing` until the transition frames are queued (`queueSpaceTransition`, called by `deliverSpaceTransition`, defers `finishSpaceAnnouncement` on every exit; `runInterestTick` clears it for planned sessions skipped as disconnected). `announcedSpaceLocked` (the `spaceAnnouncing` entry, else `sentSpace`) is what the client has been told: `SessionsInSpace`, `Space(id).Sessions`, `sessionsOutsideSpaces` and `storedWorldForSession` use it, so pushes and space events from `OnUpdates` (or another session's delivery) on the switch pass do not reach a session before its `SpaceChanged`. **Announce lock:** `Server.announceMu` (`sync.RWMutex`) orders space-dependent sends with transitions from any goroutine: `queueSpaceTransition` serializes exits and carried/entered full states first, then holds it for writing across `appendSpaceWorldFrames`, `sendReplicationStream` and `finishSpaceAnnouncement` (deferred on every exit, including errors); `Server.PushWorldData`, `Space.PushWorldData`, `SendStoredWorldData` and `Space.BroadcastServerEvent` hold it for reading across recipient (or value) selection and the enqueue, serializing before the lock where the value does not depend on the session's space. World pushes select recipients by announced space (their stream frames are not held); `Space.BroadcastServerEvent` selects by effective space (`spaceEventRecipients`: `effectiveSessionSpaceLocked`, with a plain space-0 fast path while `spacesActiveLocked` is false) and calls `holdSessionEvents` for recipients whose announced space still differs, because held events are delivered after the transition and must belong to the destination space. A session changing space twice before one pass still gets the intermediate space's events. A send therefore lands before a session's transition frames with its old-space choice (which the transition overrides) or after them with its new-space choice. Lock order: `announceMu` before `interestMu`; never take it under `interestMu`, `eventHoldMu` or a listener lock, never nest read locks (writer-preferring), and nothing called under it blocks (listener enqueues are non-blocking, held events only append). `releaseTransitionEvents` and `releaseSpaceGate` run after the write lock is released. `testHookWorldPushSelected` (tests only) runs inside `PushWorldData` between selection and enqueue. Planning and the known-set invariant still compare against `sentSpace`; the connect snapshot uses `sentSpace` (0). `Space(id).BroadcastServerEvent` sends only to announced sessions through `Listener.SendEventTo`. `sessionSpaceExplicit`, `sentSpace`, `spaceTransitionSeq`, `spaceAckPending`, `spaceAnnouncing` and `foiOrphanKnown` live under `interestMu`, hold non-zero/active entries only and are cleared in `cleanupVisibilityOnDisconnect` (and reset by physics restore). `spacesActiveLocked` (any entity outside space 0, or any explicit, announced or unacknowledged session space) gates all of it: when false, broadcast and interest replication run their single-space paths with zero plans.

**Space transitions:** when a session's space differs from `sentSpace`, `deliverSpaceTransition` replaces the pass's ordinary delivery for that session with one ordered stream batch: exits, `SpaceChanged{space, transition}`, the per-space world data (`appendSpaceWorldFrames`, see World data), full state in the current owner/public view for every Stayed (carried) entity after clearing its eventual state, entered full states, then `SpaceSettled{transition}`; ownership refresh frames are skipped for that session and frames are packed with `ConcatReliableFrames`. `transition` is a per-session counter. Interest-mode sessions without an FOI get an empty transition. Until the client's SpaceAck for the latest transition arrives, WebTransport sessions are gated: datagram-mode deltas use the stream path and each stream-sent change is merged into the tracker's `gated` set, which the ack turns into dirty state so the first datagrams carry those fields' current values even if they overtake the stream deltas. **Event lane under the gate:** the session's `golem/net` stream-only flag is on from the start of the transition until `releaseSpaceGate` succeeds, so datagram-lane server events (`SendServerEvent`, `BroadcastServerEvent`, `Space.BroadcastServerEvent`, generated `EventBroadcaster`, whose stream events also go through `SendServerEvent`/`BroadcastServerEvent`) use the ordered stream and cannot overtake the transition. It is set together with the session's event transition (`Listener.SetSessionEventTransition(sid, transition, true)`): by `beginSpaceTransitionEvents` for planned interest sessions right after unlocking `interestMu` (before `OnUpdates`), and by `deliverSpaceTransition` for WebTransport sessions before queuing anything (covers broadcast and no-FOI plans); never call the listener under `interestMu`. `releaseSpaceGate` clears only the flag (`SetSessionEventsStreamOnly(sid, false)`), keeping the transition, so later datagram event records carry it and clients drop records of an older transition (a datagram already on the wire, or held by the client during the transition, that arrives after the switch). Only non-WebTransport sessions release the gate right after the transition: a WebTransport session on `StateUpdateLaneStream` keeps it (and stream-only events) until its ack. **Event hold** (`space_events.go`): events sent before the pass that plans the transition (from `Post`, `OnTick`, `OnUpdates` or another goroutine) are held, not sent, so they follow `SpaceSettled`. Every API that can change a session's effective space checks `switchPendingLocked` (effective space ≠ `sentSpace`) under `interestMu` after its mutation and, if pending, calls `holdSessionEvents` after unlocking: `setSpacesHoldingEvents` in both `applySpaceMove` commits (the moved entities' avatar sessions via `avatarSession` and FOI sessions via `interest.Manager.AppendAnchoredSessions`), `SetSessionSpace`, `AssignFOI`, `removeFOI`, `commitAvatar` and `clearAvatarByEntity` (from `DeleteEntity`). Single-space games never hold. `beginSpaceTransitionEvents` also holds every planned interest session. `holdSessionEvents` records the session in `eventHolds` (`eventHoldMu`, a leaf lock never held with `interestMu`) and calls `Listener.HoldSessionEvents`. Flush points: `releaseTransitionEvents` right after `sendReplicationStream` queues the transition (same FIFO stream, so held events land after `SpaceSettled`, ahead of later stream-only events), unless another switch is already pending; and `releaseHeldSessionEvents`, deferred first in `runInterestTick` / `runBroadcastTick` so it runs after the replication observers, which flushes every remaining hold (sessions that did not switch after all keep their datagram lanes; disconnected ones are ignored) except that a session whose switch is still unplanned (it changed space after planning, e.g. from `OnUpdates` or an observer) is kept once (`eventHolds[sid] = true`) for the next pass. Since a pending switch is planned on the next pass, no session holds across more than one extra pass. Not covered: `CreateEntityIn` of a missing FOI anchor in another space (the switch is planned, and interest plans hold before `OnUpdates`, but events from that tick's `Post`/`OnTick` are not held); off-tick space changes racing a release may be flushed one pass early. **SpaceAck delivery:** acks bypass the bounded `msgQueue`: `enqueueSpaceAck` stores the most recent transition per session in `spaceAckInbox` (`spaceAckMu`, cannot overflow), and `drainMessages` applies the swapped-out inbox after the channel loop, so connects of that drain come first; older, unknown or disconnected-session acks are ignored by `releaseSpaceGate`. **Queued flags:** the tick-side inbox drains take no lock on quiet ticks: `spaceAckQueued`, `entityResyncQueued` and `snapshotRequestsQueued` (`atomic.Bool`) are set by the producer under the inbox lock after inserting (`storeSpaceAck`, `storeEntityResync`, `SaveSnapshotOnTick` under `lifeMu`), and `applySpaceAcks`, `takeEntityResyncs` and `drainSnapshotRequests` return at once while the flag is false, else lock, take, clear the flag and unlock; `failSnapshotRequestsLocked` (shutdown) clears its flag too. A producer racing a false read is seen on the next tick. Keep the protocol for any new tick-drained inbox (`spaceAckPending` is the gate map, not a flag). **EntityResync** (`entity_resync.go`): a client that evicted compact records held for an unspawned entity sends EntityResync; `storeEntityResync` (connection goroutine) stores the ID in the per-session set `entityResyncInbox` (`entityResyncMu`, a leaf taken after `interestMu` is released) only on `StateUpdateLaneDatagram`, for a connected WebTransport session that knows the entity (`sessionKnowsEntity`, both modes; nonpositive IDs ignored). There is no per-session cap: the set is bounded by the known set, so a burst of more than 256 evictions is kept whole. Requests are answered inside replication delivery, not in `drainMessages`: `runInterestTick` takes the inbox (`takeEntityResyncs`) before computing diffs and `runBroadcastTick` before the blind/filtered decision, where pending requests force the filtered path (`shouldFilterBroadcastLocked(..., resyncsPending)`); `replicationTick.entityResyncs` carries them. `deliverReplicationDiff` calls `appendEntityResyncFrames` right after `appendOwnershipRefreshFrames`, for datagram-capable sessions only: each requested ID in this pass's `Stayed`, sorted, gets `wrappedFullForSession` (per-tick cache, current owner/public view with the ownership re-check) on the same stream batch, after the spawn that made it known. Entities that exit this pass (deleted, out of range, moved to another space from a `Post`) get their removal instead; entered ones already get a full state; a session in a transition consumes its requests without an answer (carried and entered entities get full states). The tracker is not cleared: a later datagram may still overtake the full state, and the generated managers then re-request (see `subsystem-codegen.mdc`). **RemoveFOI orphans:** `Server.RemoveFOI` keeps the cleared known set (`interest.Manager.RemoveFOI` returns it) in `foiOrphanKnown` (entity → revision, 0 = use the live revision; `recordOrphanRemovals` stores removal revisions of orphans deleted later); disconnect and avatar-rollback paths use `removeFOI(sid, false)`, and disconnect cleanup drops the set. `AssignFOI` keeps it: in the `interestMu` section of `runInterestTick` that copies the diffs, `takeFOIOrphansLocked` adds, for every session with a diff (an FOI again) and orphans, each orphan not in that diff's Entered/Stayed/Exited to `Exited` (sorted), returns the orphan revisions per session (a separate map, since `spacePlans` is nil while spaces are inactive) and deletes the session's set; the delivery loop puts them in `plan.orphanRevisions`. Orphans the new FOI covers re-enter with a full state and no exit. This applies to single-space games too (RemoveFOI then AssignFOI used to leave out-of-range entities frozen). The no-FOI branch (`planSessionWithoutFOI`) puts the orphans in `Diff.Exited` only when the plan has a transition; without one the client keeps them frozen. Both `deliverReplicationDiff`'s exit loop and transitions use `wrappedExitWithFallback(tick, id, scope, plan.orphanRevisions)` (nil map = `wrappedExit`), which serializes exits of no-longer-registered orphans with their recorded revision.

`SetPhysicsMotionPolicy` uses optional backend atomic policy mutation without detaching entities or replacing handles. Require explicit retain/clear velocity, complete compatible controller config and navigation mode compatibility; pending frames/callbacks/restoration cannot mutate. Success synchronizes bound state and invalidates old trajectory tokens without rewinding the world. `QueuePhysicsMotionPolicy` defers contact-callback requests to the next tick and rejects stale attachment/policy generations as no-ops. Rejected backend admission leaves the old intent and bindings intact. Policy/controller intent lives in physical checkpoints; transient queue/token generations do not.

`ActivatePhysicsGeometry` is the activation-first path: after complete tick-side quarantine, activate already prepared physics source, then schedule asynchronous navigation preparation. The new physical state remains defined and quarantined after navigation failure or queue rejection; stable pending metadata survives for retry. Repeating the exact identity retries navigation without recreating physical bodies. Superseded identities cannot publish, including when a newer queue admission fails. The fully private `QueuePhysicsGeometry` path remains available separately.

Pending-world checkpoints retain accepted physical state/source leases, an explicit accepted navigation capture, desired physics/navigation bytes and stable activated/private change metadata separately. Capture fails closed while geometry is pending unless accepted-world captures are supplied. Pending restore requires `PreparePendingWorld` to privately build one `PreparedPhysicsRecovery` matching the complete checkpoint/desired fingerprints and revisions, including desired source lease ownership. It must migrate dynamic state using stable IDs and cannot mutate the live world. Root validates both candidates before publication, invalidates all pre-restore worker generations, and opens readiness only after the pair and entities/intents commit. `RestorePhysicsCheckpoint`'s first-registered defer (it runs after the Discard defer) recovers a panic: before `completed` (set right after readiness reopens, before navigation replays and `OnPhysicsRestored`) it becomes the restore error, so once anything is `published` every space faults with readiness closed; the panic is then re-raised. A panic after completion leaves the coherent restore open. Ordinary restore cannot rebuild newer desired navigation against older accepted physics.

Physical attachment and teleport admission require optional backend `PlacementValidator` capability. Synchronize before reading the current body/admitting its complete solid volume, and validate before owner, pose or entity writes. Default geometric penetration tolerance is zero; only explicit `PhysicsPlacementPolicy` permits overlap. This policy is independent of solver slop. ExternalPose bodies use the same admitted anchor path. A solver's rejected motion still returns actual accepted displacement from interactions; discard proposed navigation/traversal progress, retain actual support where valid and report Blocked/Unsupported without rolling bodies back.

**Paired physics recovery:** `CapturePhysicsCheckpoint` owns authoritative entity bytes, command owners, body bindings, source leases, desired physics/navigation captures and every navigation attachment/intent under one ID. Capture on the tick outside frames/callbacks after queued commands drain. `SavePhysicsCheckpoint` is worker-owned filesystem work: immutable content-addressed data first, manifest last. Recovery privately reconstructs fresh generational physics handles and validates entity/binding/source ownership before publication. Hosts must quiesce gameplay, disconnect clients and stop accepting new connections throughout restore. A failure after navigation publication keeps readiness closed for a complete retry; never expose mixed state. Registry replacement suppresses ordinary spawn/remove hooks, and `OnPhysicsRestored` runs only after all bindings and navigation intents are ready. Ephemeral visibility, interest, avatar/session, session-space (announced and explicit) and replication membership is reset and must be rebuilt explicitly. Multi-space restore prepares every space before committing any and faults every space when a commit fails after another succeeded. Runtime routes, contact caches and solver scratch are not persistent identities.

**Prepared physical world changes:** `QueuePhysicsGeometry` uses the bounded navigation build queue and exact key/revision/fingerprint pairing. Its tick-side callback must quarantine the full old/new physical volume and navigation bake/clearance influence before workers prepare source. Regional safety's negative bounds result confirms an intersecting barrier, not proof of complete coverage: the host owns that quarantine obligation. Publication validates current dynamic-body bounds and source ownership, loads candidate physics, atomically commits navigation, then removes the old lease. Before-publication failures remove only the candidate; unexpected cleanup failure faults readiness for paired recovery. Unaffected bodies retain handles and continue through the ordinary regional scheduler. Attached entity bodies must detach before their source lease is replaced.

`ObservePhysicsFrames` reports each attempted frame, accepted simulated duration/substeps, total/solve host duration, backend counts and errors. Actual `Intervals`/`Microsteps` and targeted `CCDCandidatePairs`/`CCDPairTests`/`CCDPredictiveContacts` survive failed attempts; predictive solver manifolds are not contact callback events. The retired global-guard counters remain separate and zero for native stepping. Its read-only callback is outside measured duration. Before sealing staged navigation, discard superseded plans and recapture safety after discard callbacks without rerunning fair scheduling; unaffected valid proposals continue.

The Box3D adapter's default physical cadence is four native steps within one 20 Hz server frame, with one velocity and one relaxation pass. Gameplay boundaries may partition shared time but may not repeat navigation fairness, multiply force duration or add a second integrator. Root depends only on portable contracts. Captured navigation/trajectory safety still validates every observed actual path and rejects the whole interacting frame on unexpected unsafe motion; normal physical contact compliance does not introduce a universal penetration-depth failure gate. World CCD and selective body bullet policy are distinct backend settings, never inferred from root navigation attachment.

Paired checkpoint envelopes stay version 1 while only space 0 has physics/navigation (version 2 adds per-space sections otherwise); embedded portable physics uses version 2 and native solver settings. `DecodePhysicsCheckpoint` must report obsolete physical payloads before unknown retired configuration fields obscure the migration error. An explicit state migration is required; ordinary entity snapshots retain their existing format.

**Surface navigation:** `SetSurfaceNavigation` (space 0) / `Space(id).SetSurfaceNavigation` is startup-only, gives each space its own backend and path/build pools (started by `Run`), and depends on `nav/surface`, never navmesh-go. `AttachNavigation` requires a registered `Spatial3DEntity` + `Position3DWriter`; `DeleteEntity` detaches before `OnRemove`. Intent and build APIs are tick-owned (startup is allowed where documented); `NavigationStats` alone reads queue counters concurrently. Independent path/build pools have bounded pending/completion queues, stable-key coalescing, priority aging, cancellation and generation checks. Workers capture values, own query scratch, and retain immutable views; they never read live entities. Complete build publication drains before path results after session messages. Managed movement runs after `OnTick`, before collision; actual-body reconciliation runs after 3D readback and before replication. Pool shutdown cancels/joins workers and discards unfinished results without publishing them.

`RegionalBackend` permits movement outside dirty tile columns while desired geometry is pending. Preserve quarantined support/destinations and wait for a changed safety token; do not busy-resubmit paths. Missing required profiles, restoration and restoration errors remain global barriers. A committed regional snapshot with pending changes is Ready/Degraded. Backends exposing only `PendingBackend` retain profile-wide pauses. Rebase unchanged support/corridors into the latest view within the fair bounded movement pass, swap all handles atomically, then release the old view. Search queries stay job-owned; following agents receive fresh lightweight queries. Initial bounded projection aligns the body through its motor; later reconciliation, retained-support replanning and traversal landings enforce `SupportTolerance` (default 0.05 world units) as a full body-to-surface distance bound. Configure tolerance against bake/body error below distinct-floor separation; do not silently snap established bodies across missing support. Steering and arrival use projected surface coordinates, while motor input, body writes, velocity and stall detection use physical coordinates. Verify attachment/generation and fresh regional safety after callbacks; check full movement segments and collision readback, not only endpoints. Float64 navigation results must fit Golem's float32 entity positions. Regional coordinate management belongs to the host.

`NavigationMotor` resolves body movement; a nil motor is navmesh-only motion. The built-in 3D overlap detector is not a character motor. `NavigationTraversal` begins a game-owned directed-link action, which advances separately, validates landing and is cancelled on interruption/removal. Ordinary moving agents do not rebuild geometry. `NavigationCheckpoint` contains stable intent identities and opaque desired-world bytes; hosts capture matching entity bytes under one checkpoint ID and commit their manifest last. Runtime locations/routes are never persistent identities.

**Staged physical navigation:** `NavigationAgentConfig.StagedPhysics` opts into `PrepareNavigationFrame` / `CommitNavigationFrame` / `AbortNavigationFrame`; it cannot combine with the synchronous motor. Prepare executes the existing fair scheduler once per server tick. Retained ordered motion segments apportion that frame's actual time; skipped scheduling must not increase simulation time. Zero-time initial projection is an explicit clearance-validated admission request. Proposed support and corner progress stay private until all body results validate. Captured safety covers every staged attachment, including idle bodies pushed by others, full paths and traversal bounds. `NavigationFrame.Frozen` requires a pre-solver stationary constraint during quarantine/readiness barriers. Unexpected unsafe physical paths fault the whole interacting frame; never roll back one character after it transferred impulses. Valid displacement that loses surface support remains the actual pose and reports Unsupported. `NavigationStagedTraversalAction` separates Prepare from exactly one Commit/Discard; all navigation support is reconciled before committed traversal callbacks run. Legacy synchronous navigation retains its existing behavior and later collision reconciliation.

**Physics ownership:** `SetPhysicsWorld` (space 0) / `Space(id).SetPhysicsWorld` installs the independent `physics3d.World` of one space before Run and transfers shutdown ownership; shutdown stops every space's navigation and then every space's physics, ascending. Root runtime imports portable contracts, never Box3D. `AttachPhysics` requires a registered 3D reader/writer, an already matching gameplay anchor and no legacy collider provider or synchronous navigation authority. Attachment owns body/collider/joint destruction on detach/delete. `PhysicsAttachmentConfig` explicitly opts into rotation, COM linear/angular velocity and activation writers; position always reads the gameplay anchor, not body origin or COM. Writers are data-only. `TeleportPhysics` preserves the body-local anchor and synchronizes query/entity visibility. `QueuePhysicsCommand` is bounded and tick-owned; callbacks queue mutations/deletions for a later tick and cannot step or directly mutate the world. The runtime validates every body and navigation path, accepts the world, writes all bindings, reconciles all support, then dispatches copied contact events. A rejected frame is never replicated; accepted callback panics follow ordinary server shutdown semantics without individual-body rollback. Physics-bound entities are skipped by legacy collision position updates/readback. Startup or tick callbacks may attach; physics event callbacks defer ownership changes. Shutdown rejects new commands, discards queued mutations and closes the owned world; optional `ShutdownWorld` supplies immutable terminal events for accepted pairs only.

Optional `NavigationBoundedTraversal` declares the complete fixed swept volume of an action; validate endpoints and every returned position against it. Unknown traversal bounds cause conservative cancellation on profile safety changes. Hosts must establish navigation quarantine before activating/removing physical geometry. `NavigationConfig.ObserveTick` observes prepare/movement/reconciliation durations and scheduled-agent counts after reconciliation; its observer must not mutate server state. Qualification must report cadence alongside timing so time budgets cannot conceal overload.

Cache successful route safety only for the same immutable route and safety token; invalidate it on release, path admission, repair and rebase. Cache location safety only for the same immutable location and safety token; clear it on every location assignment and view transfer. Recapture safety and check full physical movement segments at each acceptance boundary, including after callbacks. Quarantined agents resume acceleration from rest; unchanged distant rebases preserve their velocity.

Successful point/segment safety may be reused only for the exact same coordinates/directed endpoints and immutable safety token. Always recapture after callbacks; clear these caches on view transfer/release. `AllowPartial` corridors can rebase successfully with a Partial result: retain that status through normal following, quarantine resumption and asynchronous admission so the reachable prefix never reports the original destination as reached.

For `RetainedRegionalSafety` with `ProtectsRetiredViews`, use the independent fair retirement cursor and `RebaseAgentsPerTick`/`RebaseBudget` (64 agents/1 ms by default). Unaffected old views may continue moving only because backend checks quarantine every change since all still-leased retired generations, including full motor/traversal/collision segments. Quarantined agents still participate in retirement; failed support transfers must release their old view so quarantine can drain. Never defer rebasing with ordinary `RegionalSafety` alone: its segment check need not protect changes already published. `HasQuarantine` keeps readiness degraded during retirement even when committed and desired revisions match. Callback-induced restoration stops the remaining movement/retirement passes immediately.

`ViewRegionalSafety.ForView` narrows retirement quarantine to the agent's live generation; pending/unloaded columns still apply to every generation. Select it after every fresh capture and view transfer, including motor/traversal/collision acceptance. Never use current-view segment safety for an older view. Clear handle caches on transfer, and recheck quarantine even when the broader publication token has not changed. Backends without scoped captures retain conservative union behavior.

`ObservePathAdmission` timestamps only a successful validated worker result entering Following, including accepted partial routes. Keep unsafe completed results as owned pending routes so token changes can revalidate the actual corridor without another search. Emit admission once, only after safety clears; discard pending admission on cancellation, replacement or invalidation. It does not report failures or ordinary rebases. Consumers pair the entity/intent generation with their own request timestamp and separately record failed, cancelled, superseded and unresolved requests. Its read-only observer execution is excluded from the containing navigation phase timing but remains in the full tick duration.

Use `NavigationNow` and same-domain `Time.Sub`/`navigationSince` for navigation budgets and latency measurements. Windows uses QPC anchored to one process-local `time.Time` value; ordinary `time.Now`/`time.Since` there can quantize sub-millisecond work to zero and overrun short budgets. Never mix QPC-anchored timestamps with `time.Since` or `time.Until`. The anchored wall representation is for local interval evidence, not synchronization across processes or later civil-clock corrections. Production builds remain `CGO_ENABLED=0`; the Windows helper calls the operating system directly.

`surface.SupportIdentity` permits retaining the funnel/corner cursor during straight progress inside the same convex support polygon. A polygon change or any lateral move/motor/collision deviation requires ordinary corridor repair; compare lateral positions at entity float32 precision, not an accumulating distance tolerance. Reuse a previous physical-position projection when unchanged, or retain the exact surface returned by Move when body XZ matches at entity precision and full body-to-support distance remains within tolerance. Fresh regional safety checks still apply. Clear every retained support/projection cache on route release or rebase. Traversal collision readback also checks quarantine and declared bounds, restoring the last accepted position when safe to do so under the same intent generation.

Use optional `CorridorTransferQuery` during view transfer only for an existing route with reconciled body position, unchanged convex support since the last repair, no initial projection, no active traversal and no pending lateral deviation. It preserves the whole corridor, corner order and cursor; a transfer rejection cannot be treated as repair success. Otherwise use `RebaseQuery` repair and accept any Partial result only under the agent's partial-route policy. Swap view/query/support/route together before releasing the old lease, and invalidate all safety caches for the new scope.

**Game loop + integrated networking + world data + interest management:** `Server`, `ServerConfig` (with `Addr`, `Path`, `StaticDir`, `Transport`, `StateUpdateLane`, `CellSize` for FOI, optional `LogReplicationStats` (or env `GOLEM_LOG_REPLICATION_STATS=1`) for once-per-second replication batching / wire-size diagnostics), `TickFunc` `func(dt float64, s *Server)`, `ContactFunc` `func(contacts []CollisionContact)`, `OnTick` / `OnUpdates` / `OnContact`, `SetRemovalSerializer`, `SetCollisionBackend`, `Run` with fixed timestep. **Entity world API on `Server`:** `CreateEntity(e, owner ...int64) error` (unowned vs owned via optional session ID) and `CreateEntityIn`, `DeleteEntity`, `EntitySpace`, `SetEntitySpace`, `Get`, `Owner`, `SetOwner`, `All`, `Len`, `SnapshotAll`—all delegate to an internal `*registry.Registry` (`reg` field, unexported). Optional **`ServerBinder`** (`BindServer(*Server)`): `CreateEntity` calls it after ID assignment and before registry insertion, so `OnSpawn` can use a bound server; snapshot restore goes through `CreateEntityIn` with the recorded space and gets the same binding. CreateEntity uses `AddWithoutSpawnIn`/`AddOwnedWithoutSpawnIn` then collider registration then `registry.NotifySpawn` so spawn hooks observe bound server, space and registered colliders. **Session avatars:** `SpawnAvatar(sess, e, AvatarOptions{FOIRadius, FOIMargin, Space})` does `CreateEntityIn(opts.Space, e, sess.ID)`, assigns FOI when `FOIRadius > 0` (errors if interest is disabled or radius is negative; `0` disables FOI), and records concurrency-safe `sessionID↔entityID` indexes under `avatarMu` (not held across registry hooks or interest ops). FOI goes through `AssignFOI`/`RemoveFOI` (`interestMu`). Lock order when both are needed: `interestMu` before `avatarMu`. `Avatar(sessionID)` and generic `AvatarOf[T](s, sessionID)` look up the bound entity. Duplicate `SpawnAvatar` for a live avatar is rejected (no replace). On disconnect, user `OnDisconnect` runs first (avatar still readable; listener always delivers a non-nil `*Session`), then automatic `RemoveFOI` (when interest enabled) and reaping of any avatar still bound to the session — including a replacement spawned inside the callback — so avatars cannot leak past disconnect. Avatar rollback and disconnect use `Server.DeleteEntity`, removing registered 2D and 3D colliders independently before `OnRemove`. Mid-session `DeleteEntity` clears matching avatar indexes in O(1); unlinking only removes matching bidirectional entries, leaving pending reservations to the reservation helpers. `SetOwner` changes command authority only — it does not transfer avatar identity. `NewServer` always creates an internal `golem/net.Listener` wired to that registry, with `WrapEntityUpdate` as the reliable stream entity message wrapper and a world snapshot closure. Automatic incremental entity replication uses `StateUpdateLaneDatagram` per session by default: WebTransport receives compact eventual-state datagrams while WebSocket fallback sessions receive equivalent wrapped deltas on the reliable stream. `StateUpdateLaneStream` keeps incremental updates on the reliable stream for every session. Datagram state updates fall oversized/full-state payloads back to the reliable stream. Reliable-stream chunk statistics use each session's actual transport. When `CellSize > 0`, an `interest.Manager` is created and interest management is enabled (entity snapshots on connect are deferred). When `Addr` is set, `Run` starts both the tick loop and the built-in HTTP server plus the selected transport endpoint (WebTransport by default, WebSocket when configured with `StateUpdateLaneStream`). When `Addr` is empty, `Run` only ticks; the game mounts `Handler()` on its own router or HTTP/3 server and may mount `WebSocketHandler()` on a separate HTTP server. `Run` shutdown closes sessions accepted through either handler.

**Interest management:** when enabled (`CellSize > 0`), `AssignFOI(sessionID, entityID, radius, margin)` and `RemoveFOI(sessionID)` control per-session fields of interest. All Server FOI / visibility / broadcast-known access (`AssignFOI`, `RemoveFOI`, visibility group APIs, `SessionsKnowing` known-set reads, and the `UpdateGrid`+`ComputeDiffsFiltered` critical section) is guarded by `interestMu` so gameplay can call these APIs off the tick goroutine; `interestMu` is not held across network sends or registry hooks. `runInterestTick` copies filtered diffs under the mutex, then flushes and sends unlocked. Entered entities get `FullUpdate`, stayed+dirty get delta or compact datagram record, exited get removal. Global entities (`IsGlobal() == true`) bypass distance but still honor visibility-group membership. Sessions without FOI still receive no entities (including grouped globals) but still receive space transitions, which exit what they knew before `RemoveFOI`; after `AssignFOI` the first pass exits what the new FOI does not cover. Each session replicates only entities of its space (`Diff.Space` for FOI sessions). When interest is **not** enabled, `runBroadcastTick` uses per-session known-set diffs when visibility groups, spaces (`spacesActiveLocked`) or known-set reconciliation require it; `computeBroadcastDiff` desires only entities in the session's space that visibility allows. Entity spaces change only before `Run` or on the tick, so while `reg.SpacesInUse()` is false single-space work skips per-entity spaces with one space check per session (a plan or `sentSpace` other than 0 desires nothing): filtered broadcast still reads `AppendSpaced` into the reused `replicationScratch.spaced` (allocation-free; the registry skips lookups) and passes `computeBroadcastDiff` `plain = !SpacesInUse()` read after it, and the connect snapshot (connection goroutine) reads plain `reg.All()`, rechecking `SpacesInUse` after the read, else `AppendSpaced` with a per-entity compare. The choice keys on `SpacesInUse`, not `spacesActiveLocked`, because session space maps change off-tick. `releaseSpaced` (deferred in filtered broadcast) clears the scratch's entity pointers at the end of the pass. It keeps a blind fast path that wraps once and calls `SendBatch` per session when every session already knows the live public set, no space bookkeeping is active and neither owner-scoped entities nor ownership refreshes require filtering.

**Replication implementation:** `loop.go` owns orchestration and known-set policy. `replication.go` shares filtered/interest flush preparation, immutable payload caches, per-recipient delivery, removal serialization, and stream submission counting. Interest captures copied diffs and ownership refreshes before `FlushAll`; filtered broadcast captures recipients/prepared eventual frames before `OnUpdates`, then computes live diffs and captures refreshes after it. Keep those timing differences and the blind fast path separate. Actual deletions use registry removal revisions; visibility/FOI exits use the current revision (zero when absent). `owner_replication.go` owns recipient view selection with separate authoritative/public caches. `eventual_state.go` owns dirty merging and one packing/delivery loop for cached or prepared frames; successful owned datagram submission transfers its buffer, while tracker descriptors and future packet buffers remain independent. Production replication reuses `golem/net` framing/budget helpers; it does not own wire protocol or ACK scheduling.

**Visibility groups:** `Server` always owns one `golem/visibility.Manager` (no policy mirroring into interest). Public APIs: `JoinVisibilityGroup`, `LeaveVisibilityGroup`, `SetEntityVisibilityGroup` (empty group = public). Concurrency-safe under `interestMu` and never wait on network I/O. Policy is point-in-time: blind-vs-filtered decisions and connect-snapshot allowed-ID selection (entities in the session's announced space, 0 at connect, that visibility allows) linearize under `interestMu`, then the mutex is released before `FullUpdate` / `SendBatch` / writes. A mutation after that decision takes effect on the next replication pass via known-set enter/exit (already selected/queued frames are not retroactively revoked). `SessionsKnowing` consults actual known sets in both modes, never raw membership. Broadcast known state is cleared if SendBatch observes a disconnected session after enter decisions. Group/tombstone metadata for deleted entities is retained until that tick's removal decisions are handled, then cleared. Disconnect clears session membership and broadcast known state (FOI cleanup remains in avatar disconnect).

**Owner-only vars:** entities implementing `OwnerScopedEntity` (generated when YAML has `visibility: owner`) send authoritative full/delta to the current owner and `PublicFullUpdate` / `PublicReplicationMask`-filtered deltas to everyone else (zero public mask → no delta). Blind broadcast is disabled when any live entity is owner-scoped or an ownership refresh is pending. Connect snapshots use the same owner-vs-public selection after visibility-group filtering. `SetOwner` updates command authority immediately and, when the owner actually changes, calls the entity's optional `registry.StateRevisionBumper` (generated entities implement it) so the refresh or transition full state carries a newer revision than any record serialized for the previous view; Owner lookup, registry mutation, and refresh coalescing are atomic under `interestMu` (before registry lock) relative to other `Server.SetOwner` calls, and locks are never held across sends/hooks. `SetOwner` changes the registry owner before it bumps the revision; replication relies on that order: it chooses a view (`viewForSession`) and serializes later without `interestMu`, so after producing owner-view bytes of an owner-scoped entity it re-checks `sessionOwnsEntity` and uses the public view when ownership moved meanwhile (bytes stamped with the bumped revision always fail the check; earlier bytes are older than the refresh). Places: `wrappedFullForRecipient` (used by `wrappedFullForSession`; authoritative bytes that fail are not cached, cache hits are re-checked too), `fullUpdateForSession` (connect snapshot), the ownership grant (`appendOwnershipRefreshFrames` grants only while the snapshot's `newOwner` is still the owner; a later change queued its own refresh) and eventual frames (`eventualStateFrame.ownerScoped`, set in `eventualFrameForChange`; `sendEventualFrames` replaces a private change whose recipient lost ownership with `publicEventualFrame`, the `PublicReplicationMask` change that `markSent` records so retries stay public; prepared frames only exist without owner-scoped deltas). Stream deltas and spawn bytes come from `FlushAll`, before view selection, so they need no re-check. It queues a coalesced replication refresh for owner-scoped entities (original old known owner → public full; final new known owner → authoritative full; same-tick A→…→A cancels; `0` clears ownership). Refresh is gated by known-set stay (entered already got correct full; exited get removal) and clears that session/entity eventual tracker so pending private deltas cannot survive a full clear/grant. Public eventual frames reapply `PublicReplicationMask` at serialize time (defense in depth against stale private bits after loss/requeue merge). Eventual-state cache keys include a public bit so private bytes are never reused for non-owners. Persistence/`SnapshotAll` stay authoritative. `visibility: owner` is replication redaction, not command authorization.

**Collision integration:** opt-in via `SetCollisionBackend(b collision.Backend)` / `SetCollision3DBackend` (space 0) or `Space(id).SetCollisionBackend` / `SetCollision3DBackend`. `stepCollision` runs after `onTick` and before the flush, 2D then 3D: for each space with a backend in ascending order, (1) `backend.Update` for that space's live entities (the plain `reg.All()` snapshot feeds only space 0 while `SpacesInUse` is false; otherwise one `AppendSpaced` snapshot per phase, filtered per space), (2) `backend.Step(dt)` → contacts, (3) `backend.ReadBack` writes positions back to `PositionWriter` / 3D writers only for entities of that space; physics-attached entities are skipped in both directions. Then once per tick, with contacts merged across spaces in space order (the single backend's slice is passed through unchanged; several spaces reuse `contactScratch` / `contactScratch3D`): (4) `onContact` / `onContact3D` when contacts were found, (5) 2D-only `dispatchContactEvents` when enabled and any 2D backend stepped (pre-existing; not part of schema-collider 3D parity). Pair maps stay on `Server`, keyed by entity ID: IDs are unique and pairs never cross spaces, so at the next collision step a pair split by a move exits, while a pair whose entities moved together (and still touch) keeps its history and reports stay (a deliberate choice). **Named layers:** `CollisionLayers` (2D) and `CollisionLayers3D` (3D) with `Bind`/`Define`/`SetCollides`/`Add`/`Set`/`Remove`/`Layer`/`Mask`/`MaskFor`; store via `SetCollisionLayers` / `SetCollisionLayers3D` (space 0) or the `Space(id)` setters. Generated `EnableCollision`/`EnableCollision3D` call `MustCollisionBackend`/`MustCollisionBackend3D` so nil backends panic before installing helpers. **Automatic schema colliders:** `ColliderProvider` and `ColliderProvider3D` register/unregister independently on the helpers of the entity's space (`runtimeOf`) when both are set, including entity-setup rollback; `CreateEntity` (including snapshot restore) registers after successful `AddWithoutSpawn` and before `NotifySpawn`/`OnSpawn`; `DeleteEntity` removes after avatar index cleanup and before registry deletion/`OnRemove`. Missing layer helpers skip registration without panicking; call EnableCollision helpers (or setters) before spawning/snapshot-loading collidable entities. Failed duplicate CreateEntity does not register a collider. **Spatial queries** (`spatial.go`): `OverlapBox`/`OverlapCircle`/`OverlapBox3D`/`OverlapSphere` (and cast helpers) return entity IDs from space 0's collision backend; every query is mirrored on `Space(id)` for that space's backend (no hits when the space has none); free function `OverlapOfType[T Entity](s, entityIDs)` resolves those IDs via `Server.Get`, retains only entities satisfying `T`, preserves input order, skips missing IDs, returns nil when input or result is empty, and always panics on nil `*Server` with `golem: OverlapOfType: Server must be non-nil` (including empty ID lists)—compose as `OverlapOfType[*Mob](s, s.OverlapCircle(...))` rather than adding geometry-specific generics.

**Per-entity collision events:** opt-in via `EnableContactEvents()`, which sets `contactEventsEnabled = true` and allocates `triggerPairs map[[2]int64]struct{}` and `solidPairs map[[2]int64]struct{}` (both fields on `Server`, defined in `loop.go`). Requires a collision backend. Six opt-in entity interfaces in `golem/collision_events.go`: `TriggerEnter`, `TriggerStay`, `TriggerExit` (method arg: `other Entity`); `CollisionEnter`, `CollisionStay`, `CollisionExit` (method args: `other Entity, normal CollisionVec2, depth float64`). `dispatchContactEvents(contacts []collision.Contact)` in `collision_events.go`: splits contacts into `currentTrigger` (Depth==0) and `currentSolid` (Depth!=0) maps keyed by `contactKey(a,b int64) [2]int64` (canonical min-first pair); fires Enter/Stay/Exit by comparing current maps against `triggerPairs`/`solidPairs`; swaps both maps only after trigger transitions (including exits) and then solid transitions finish. The shared pair traversal resolves both endpoints before either callback; typed emitters retain trigger/solid callback signatures. **Normal convention:** `Contact.Normal` is the push direction for `Contact.A` (unit vector from B toward A); entity B receives the negated vector so both sides get a normal pointing away from the other. **nil other:** if `reg.Get` returns false for a partner, `other` is passed as nil; the receiver entity still gets the callback (guarded by its own `okA`/`okB`). Both sides can be stale simultaneously — neither fires. `contactKey` returns `{min(a,b), max(a,b)}`.

Each tick order: **`s.tick++`** → **`OnTickStart(tick)`** → `drainPhysics` (each space's due queued physics commands, space 0 then ascending) → **`drainAsyncCallbacks()`** (bounded `SubmitTask` completions and `Server.Post` callbacks under one `AsyncCallbacksPerTick` budget; round-robin starts completion-first and persists across ticks) → **`drainMessages()`** (OnConnect / OnMessage / OnDisconnect) → `prepareNavigation` (each space's navigation build publication and path results, ascending) → **`reg.TickAll(dt)`** → `onTick(dt, s)` → `stepSpaces` (per space ascending: physics step with its own staged navigation frame, else navigation movement/motor; space moves rejected for the loop) → collision steps (per space in ascending order: Update own entities → Step → ReadBack; then contact callbacks/events once with merged contacts, 2D then 3D) → `reconcileNavigation` (each space's body reconciliation, ascending) → flush + broadcast → `drainSnapshotRequests` (pending `SaveSnapshotOnTick` captures, arrival order) → **`OnTickEnd(tick, wall)`**. `tick` is a `uint64` field on `Server`, incremented at the top of each tick (1-based, single goroutine). `Tick()` returns it for game logic. Hook callbacks run on the tick goroutine so trace regions and profiling labels nest with simulation work.

**Bounded tasks / Post / Run lifecycle:** `Server.SubmitTask` runs work on an internal Pond v2 pool (`TaskWorkers` default 4, `TaskQueueCapacity` default 256, `pond.WithContext(runCtx)`); completions use an independent bounded queue (`TaskCompletionQueueCapacity` default 256). `Server.Post` enqueues a tick callback (never inline). Acceptance is gated by a linearizable `created → running → stopping → stopped` lifecycle under `lifeMu` (shared with non-blocking `postQueue` sends and `TrySubmitErr`). `preRunCaptureMu` (RWMutex): snapshot captures that observe `lifecycleCreated` take its read lock under `lifeMu` and hold it through their synchronous capture; `Run` takes and releases its write lock after releasing `lifeMu` (never under it: capture runs entity `FullUpdate`, which may call `Post`) and before the listener and first tick. `Run` is single-use (`ErrServerAlreadyRun` on concurrent/repeated calls) and owns an independent run context + pool (caller ctx is not the Pond parent, so shutdown can cancel then `StopAndWait`). An already-cancelled caller `Run` context linearizes `stopping` under `lifeMu` before `runLoop` or Post/SubmitTask acceptance; live caller cancellation still links via `context.AfterFunc` after acceptance opens. `Post` / `SubmitTask` return `ErrServerNotRunning` before `Run`, once shutdown linearizes `stopping`, and after `Run` returns; full queues return `ErrPostQueueFull` / `ErrTaskQueueFull` without blocking. When the completion queue is full, finished workers select on enqueue vs run cancellation (backpressure, no drop while running). Effective work context is `WithCancel(callerCtx)` with run-context cancellation linked via AfterFunc (already-/queue-cancelled callers are visible synchronously when work starts); a non-nil work error is preserved, else a cancelled effective context surfaces as the completion error; panics are recovered in Golem's wrapper, logged with stack, and delivered as errors wrapping `ErrTaskPanicked` with exactly one tick completion while running. Ownership: workers must not touch Server/registry/visibility/session/tick-owned state — only completion/`Post` callbacks may. Config also includes `PostQueueCapacity` (default 1024) and `AsyncCallbacksPerTick` (default 256); zero means default, negative panics in `NewServer`. Shutdown order: stop accepting → cancel run/worker context → wait for Pond (queued-not-started tasks are not executed; running tasks receive cancellation) → discard queued completions/posts → stopped. No callback runs after the tick loop exits; work that ignores cancellation can delay `Run` return. **Observability:** `Server.TaskStats()` returns `TaskStats` with live gauges `QueuedTasks`/`RunningTasks` (Pond `WaitingTasks`/`RunningWorkers` when the pool exists) and `QueuedCompletions`/`QueuedPosts` (channel `len`; all four approximate under concurrency), plus Golem atomics `TasksAccepted`, `TasksFinished` (work return/panic only), `TaskCompletionsExecuted` (tick callback actually ran; shutdown discard keeps finished≠executed), `TasksRejectedFull`, `TasksCancelled` (queued-not-started discarded on shutdown, or work whose effective context ended — each accepted task at most once), `TaskPanics`, `PostsAccepted`, `PostsExecuted`, `PostsRejectedFull`. Do not mirror Pond submitted/dropped cumulatives: Pond counts full-queue drops in submitted. **Accepted-before-work:** each successful `TrySubmitErr` opens a per-submission start gate only after `TasksAccepted` increments under `lifeMu` (rejected submissions never open the gate), so finish/cancel/panic cannot race ahead of accepted; the tick is never blocked on queue capacity.

**Session event queue:** `OnConnect`, `OnMessage`, and `OnDisconnect` (and internal eventual feedback events) are enqueued from connection goroutines (via internal `enqueueConnect` / `enqueueMessage` / `enqueueDisconnect` hooks installed on the `Listener` in `NewServer`) and dispatched on the tick goroutine during `drainMessages`. The user-facing `OnConnect(fn)` / `OnMessage(fn)` / `OnDisconnect(fn)` methods **only store `fn`** — they no longer call `s.listener.On*`. This makes it safe to call `CreateEntity`, `DeleteEntity`, entity `Set*` methods, and all other `Server` APIs from all three callbacks without any additional synchronization. After the user `OnDisconnect` callback, the server runs avatar auto-cleanup (`RemoveFOI` / delete avatar entity / clear indexes). Events are queued in a buffered channel of capacity 4096; if the queue is full the event is dropped and a warning is logged (the connection goroutine is never blocked). Each event fires up to one tick after it occurs — `OnConnect` in particular fires on the tick after the world-state snapshot is delivered, which shifts when the player entity appears relative to the interest system's first visibility pass. `OnUpgrade` is unaffected and still runs synchronously before the WebSocket is accepted.

**External router support:** `Handler()` returns the configured-primary transport endpoint for mounting on a custom router (e.g. Chi alongside REST routes). `WebSocketHandler()` always returns a fallback handler backed by the same listener and game loop. `OnUpgrade(func(*http.Request) (any, error))` provides pre-upgrade auth; the returned value populates `Session.Data` before `OnConnect` fires, and a non-nil error rejects with HTTP 401. For token-in-query-param upgrades, use `golem/auth.UpgradeHandler` (stdlib-only helper; does not import `golem`). When using WebTransport on a caller-owned server, pair `Handler()` with `WebTransportServer(...)` on HTTP/3. Session lifecycle hooks: `OnConnect`, `OnMessage`, `OnDisconnect`, and `OnDatagram` for the lossy WebTransport lane. Direct-send helpers: `Send(sessionID, data)`, `Broadcast(data)`, `SendUnreliable(data)` / `BroadcastUnreliable(data)` when the target transport supports datagrams.

**Realtime bootstrap:** `RealtimeConfigHandler(RealtimeConfigOptions)` serves the primary `transport`/`url`, primary-only certificate hashes and eventual ACK interval, plus optional `fallback: {transport:"websocket",url}` from `WebSocketFallbackURL` when WebTransport is primary. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`; configuring a fallback for a non-WebTransport primary returns HTTP 500 rather than advertising an invalid chain.

**`servermsg.go`**: `WrapEntityUpdate` wraps `EntityUpdate` bytes in a `ServerMessage` envelope (proto field 1, length-delimited). `WrapWorldUpdate` wraps `WorldUpdate` bytes in a `ServerMessage` envelope (proto field 2, length-delimited). The `Listener` applies `WrapEntityUpdate` transparently via its `messageWrapper`; world frames bypass it entirely and use `WrapWorldUpdate` via the world snapshot closure or `BroadcastRaw`. `WrapSpaceChanged` (field 4), `WrapSpaceSettled` (field 5) and `WrapWorldCleared` (field 6) wrap the space envelope messages; zero values are omitted.

**`export.go`** re-exports `Entity`, opt-in entity interfaces `Ticker`, `Spawner`, `Remover`, `PositionWriter`, `Session` (from `golem/net`), `InterestManager`, `InterestFOI`, `InterestDiff` (from `golem/interest`), `WorldData`/`WorldStore` (from `golem/world`), and collision types including `CollisionBackend`, `CollisionShape`, `CollisionLayers`, `CollisionBackend3D`, `CollisionShape3D`, `CollisionLayers3D`, and related shape/contact aliases so consumers use a single **`golem`** import path. Six per-entity collision event interfaces (`TriggerEnter`, `TriggerStay`, `TriggerExit`, `CollisionEnter`, `CollisionStay`, `CollisionExit`) are defined directly in `golem/collision_events.go`. **`Registry` / `FlushResult` / `NewRegistry` are not re-exported**—use `golem.Server` for gameplay or import `golem/registry` for low-level embedding.

**World data** (`Server.World`): `*world.Store` is always initialized. `Server.PushWorldData(name) error` broadcasts one world type to all sessions (except those whose announced space's store overrides the name; see Per-space world data); returns nil when the name is missing or networking is inactive, and returns a non-nil error if `MarshalUpdate` fails. `Server.SendWorldData(sessionID, data) error` is the primary per-session API: it marshals the supplied value directly (no `World.Set`), wraps with `WrapWorldUpdate`, and sends on the reliable stream to that session only — two sessions may receive different values with the same `WorldName`. Nil and typed-nil `data` return `golem: SendWorldData requires non-nil world data`. `Server.SendStoredWorldData(sessionID, name) error` matches `PushWorldData`'s missing-name nil/no-op, then delegates to `SendWorldData`. Marshal, oversize (256 KiB reliable frame cap), and disconnected-session errors propagate. `ServerConfig.WorldSnapshotExclude` lists names omitted from the connect snapshot; `NewServer` copies/normalizes the slice (drops empties/duplicates) into an immutable-after-construction exclude set used by the world snapshot closure. Excluded entries remain in `Server.World`; callers own on-demand delivery. Large embedded maps should use `map_url`. On connect, the listener still sends (non-excluded) world data before entity snapshots, in deterministic sorted name order.

**Per-space world data** (`space_world.go`): `Space(id).World()` returns that space's `*world.Store`, created lazily in `Server.spaceWorlds` under the leaf lock `spaceWorldMu` (never held while taking `interestMu`) and shared by every handle; space 0 has its own store, distinct from `Server.World`. `spaceWorldsUsed` (atomic, set on first creation) gates every per-space check, so games without per-space stores keep the old paths and bytes. Values resolve per space: the space's store, else `Server.World`. The connect snapshot (`worldSnapshotFrames`) is the old `MarshalAllExcept` output while no per-space store defines a name; otherwise it sends, for the sorted union of `Server.World` names and all per-space names, the space-0 resolution or `WrapWorldCleared` when nothing resolves (clearing values a reconnecting client kept from its previous space). A transition into X sends, for the sorted union of names defined in any per-space store, X's resolution or `WorldCleared`; names only in `Server.World` are not resent. `WorldSnapshotExclude` names are caller-delivered everywhere: neither the connect snapshot nor a transition sends or clears them (a clear could wipe a value the caller delivered before the pass). `Space(id).PushWorldData(name)` sends the space's own value to `SessionsInSpace(id)` via `Listener.SendRawTo` (nil when the store or name is missing; marshal and size errors propagate). `PushWorldData` skips sessions whose announced space's store defines the name, and `SendStoredWorldData` resolves the session's announced space (`announcedSpaceLocked`). Pushes are ordered with transitions from any goroutine through `announceMu` (see Session spaces).

**`golem/world`** package: `Data` interface (`WorldName`, `MarshalUpdate`), thread-safe `Store` (`Set`, `Get`, sorted `Names`, `MarshalAll` / `MarshalAllExcept(exclude)` in sorted name order). Pure data/serialization only — no session or network policy. No dependencies on `golem`, `golem/net`, or `golem/registry`.

Depends on **`golem/registry`** for entity storage, **`golem/net`** for networking, **`golem/interest`** for FOI management, **`golem/visibility`** for named group policy, **`golem/world`** for world data, and **`github.com/demiurgos-hub/golem-engine/golem/collision`** (local sibling module, resolved by `go.work`) for the collision backend interface; does **not** implement `Entity` or registry internals—those stay in `golem/registry/`.

**`golem/debug`** subpackage: `StartPprof(addr string) error` starts a pprof HTTP debug server on `addr` (e.g. `"127.0.0.1:6060"`). Uses a dedicated `http.ServeMux` — `http.DefaultServeMux` is not touched. All pprof handlers (`/debug/pprof/`, `/cmdline`, `/profile`, `/symbol`, `/trace`) are mounted explicitly via the named `net/http/pprof` import. Since the import lives only in `golem/debug`, production binaries that do not import it never register pprof handlers. Call from `main` behind a flag; never import in production builds.
